 ## Background
-The use of wavelets for volume rendering and compression emerged in the late 1990s and early 2000s as a solution to the memory bandwidth bottleneck in GPU-based direct volume rendering (DVR). Early work by Guthe et al. [S2, unofficial] demonstrated hierarchical wavelet representations allowing compression ratios of 30:1 without noticeable artifacts. The Laplacian pyramid approach [GY95] described in [S1] uses low-pass filtering and delta computation for on-the-fly reconstruction. Wetekam et al. [WSKW05] proposed an octree-based multiresolution approach with FPGA hardware decoding [S1]. Wu et al. [WQ05] introduced a modified 3-D dyadic wavelet transform with Rice coding for medical images [S1]. Shigeru Muraki [S2, unofficial] presented 3D orthogonal wavelet approximations using tensor products. More recent developments include the use of Shearlets [S1] for directional features and DTCWT [S1] for shift invariance. The field continues to evolve with neural field representations requiring specialized mask compression [S1][S2, unofficial].
